<h2>How to use this data (and how not to)</h2>
<p>Read these figures as a map of where India's spice trade goes, not as a price list. Three cautions keep the numbers honest:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Shares measure destination, not price.</strong> A market's share is its slice of India's declared export value for that HS line. A high share tells you a market buys a lot; it says nothing about what any single buyer pays or what a quote for your grade should be.</li>
  <li><strong>Unit values are averages across everything.</strong> Dividing a line's total value by its total weight blends whole and ground product, every grade and every contract across the year. It's useful for comparing how value-dense one spice is against another. It isn't a benchmark for negotiating one shipment.</li>
  <li><strong>Codes and calendars differ.</strong> WITS reports several lines under legacy codes (0904.20 for dried chilli, 0909.30 for cumin, 0908.30 for cardamom, 0909.20 for coriander), while current nomenclature splits whole from crushed or ground. These are calendar-year figures, so they won't line up exactly with India's April–March fiscal-year releases.</li>
</ul>
<p>For anything commercial, send your specification and destination port and ask for a current quote. The data tells you which lanes are deep; the quote tells you what your cargo costs.</p>

<h2>China: the market most buyers overlook</h2>
<p>If you asked most buyers to name India's top spice export destination, the Gulf would likely come up first. The data says otherwise for the two largest spice categories by value: China is the clear #1 market for both dried red chilli and cumin, importing more than triple the next-largest chilli market and more than 50% ahead of the next-largest cumin market. Any serious Indian spice sourcing strategy that ignores China is missing the single largest buyer relationship in the category.</p>
<p>China's pull is narrow, though. It takes about 35% of India's chilli export value and about 18% of cumin, but it doesn't appear in the 2024 top ten for turmeric, cardamom, coriander or black pepper. Its weight in the overall picture comes almost entirely from those two high-volume lines.</p>

<h2>The Gulf's real strength: cardamom, and high-value spices generally</h2>
<p>Where the Gulf does dominate is cardamom — the UAE and Saudi Arabia alone account for roughly 61% of India's cardamom export value, rising above 70% with Kuwait, Iraq and Oman included, driven by cardamom's central role in Gulf culinary tradition, particularly qahwa (Arabic coffee). Turmeric shows a similar Gulf-and-South-Asia lean, with the UAE and Bangladesh essentially tied for the top spot.</p>

<h2>Two markets that show up for every spice</h2>
<p>Across all six culinary spices in this overview, only the United States and the UAE appear in every 2024 top-ten list. The US ranks first for black pepper, third for chilli, turmeric, cardamom and coriander, and fourth for cumin. The UAE ranks first for turmeric and cardamom, second for coriander, third for cumin, fourth for black pepper and eighth for chilli. Bangladesh and Malaysia come close, each appearing in five of the six lists.</p>
<p>For a buyer, this matters in two ways. If you import into the US or the UAE, the lanes from India are established across the whole spice range, so consolidating several spices with one exporter is a realistic option. And if your market isn't on these lists, that doesn't rule it out; it means you should check documentation and residue requirements early rather than assume they're routine.</p>

<h2>Volume vs. value: not the same ranking</h2>
<p>Cardamom is worth a specific callout: at only about 9,033 MT of total 2024 volume, it's a tiny fraction of chilli's or cumin's tonnage, yet its roughly USD 22.5/kg average unit value makes it the highest-value spice per kilogram in this comparison by a wide margin. If you're evaluating spice categories by opportunity rather than raw export value, unit economics matter as much as total trade size.</p>
