<p>Chilli is the opposite case to cardamom: the largest line by both value and tonnage, at a modest average value per kilogram. Black pepper sits between them, with a tonnage close to cardamom's but a far lower value density.</p>

<h2>Black pepper and coriander: smaller lines, different buyers</h2>
<p>Whole black pepper exports were about USD 55.6 million in 2024, and the buyer list looks unlike any other spice here. The United States takes about 26% of value, followed by Sweden and Germany, with Japan, the Netherlands, the UK and Italy also in the top ten. It's a Western, quality-led buyer base rather than a volume one.</p>
<p>Coriander seed was about USD 78.0 million, led by Malaysia and the UAE, which together take about 36% of value. Its average unit value is the lowest in this comparison, which makes freight a bigger share of landed cost per kilogram than it is for cardamom or pepper.</p>

